  "id": "COMPOUND:18418 - PROTEIN:6326",
  "verbalization": "The visible evidence suggests a potential relationship between Vidalol B and Phosphodiesterase 6C, mediated through the target SNU-387. Vidalol B is reported to interact with SNU-387, which is a target of multiple compounds, including 10,22-Dihydroxy-7,7,18,18-tetramethyl-8,13,17-trioxapentacyclo[12.8.0.03,12.04,9.016,21]docosa-1(22),3(12),4(9),10,14,16(21),19-heptaen-2-one and 3-Bromo-4-(2,3-Dibromo-4,5-Dihydroxybenzyl)-5-Methoxymethylpyrocatecol. All of these compounds are reported to have a target of Phosphodiesterase 6C. This suggests that Vidalol B may act through a common mechanism, possibly by inhibiting Phosphodiesterase 6C, which is involved in the breakdown of cyclic nucleotides and has been implicated in various physiological processes. The evidence is consistent with a direct interaction between Vidalol B and Phosphodiesterase 6C, supported by multiple independent pathways. The mechanism is not fully elucidated, but the shared target of multiple compounds with Phosphodiesterase 6C indicates a plausible biological role. The evidence is robust, but further experimental validation is needed to confirm the exact mode of action and the extent of the interaction.",
